<h2>The sweet spot counter-argument</h2>

<p>The obvious counter to riding low zone 2 on six hours a week is the existence of sweet spot training. The argument is straightforward: if you only have six hours, riding at 85 to 90 percent of your threshold for most of them will raise your FTP faster than riding easy.</p>

<p>This is entirely true for the first four to six weeks.</p>

<p>Sweet spot training on low volume builds a floor quickly. But it also lowers your ceiling. If every ride is medium-hard, you never develop the sheer mitochondrial density that comes from high-volume zone 2, and you are never fresh enough to execute the >95 percent HRmax sessions that actually move your top-end.</p>

<p>By week eight of a sweet spot plan, the rapid FTP gains stop. You plateau, your heart rate decoupling increases, and you carry a permanent, low-level fatigue into every session. The WorldTour architecture—strict polarization, focused high intensity, and earned recovery—takes longer to show its first results. But it continues scaling for years, keeping you fresh enough to actually enjoy the process.</p>
